{"type":"text","timestamp":1780750720836,"sessionID":"ses_162fcfe43ffedLCZB78Jqbs18W","part":{"id":"prt_e9d0392e2001Eid8jui5mvlcs6","messageID":"msg_e9d037e7c0010oAvoLG710iM9M","sessionID":"ses_162fcfe43ffedLCZB78Jqbs18W","type":"text","text":"The required code-quality convention confirms the A1 coupling row and the adapter/intrinsic rules: explicit declarations are required, adapter/intrinsic thresholds are LOW at <= 5 contracts/domains when all references are subordinate.","time":{"start":1780750717667,"end":1780750720833},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"msg_0ccf1fa93a57f80f016a24197da38081948d442990c6dd551b","phase":"commentary"}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750720905,"sessionID":"ses_162fcfe43ffedLCZB78Jqbs18W","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_1VJBO7bnvA63RTBeoga6T89p","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","offset":600,"limit":600},"output":"<path>/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n600: - editing files is not Tier-3\n601: - running tests is not Tier-3\n602: - opening **draft** PRs is not Tier-3\n603: - public promotion and other outward-facing actions follow the tiered-approval workflow\n604: \n605: ## Pause For Refactor\n606: \n607: This section owns the pause state model for existing-code refactor work discovered during an implementation WU. For the strategy boundary, cite `conventions/feature-development-workflow.md` `## Refactoring out of scope`; do not restate that rule elsewhere in this workflow.\n608: \n609: - trigger: an auditor, reviewer, or implementation step identifies existing-code structural refactor work that is outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for the current WU, and material to continuing safely.\n610: - pause evidence: record the current ticket id, branch, head SHA, worktree path, dirty status, failing or residual report paths, and the reason the refactor is outside touched ownership or requires decomposition.\n611: - ticket/backend action: use the selected ticket backend to comment the pause evidence on the current ticket and apply the available pause/blocking status or label; if no status exists, use a clear blocking comment plus link relation.\n612: - refactor-ticket filing: file a linked refactor-ticket with the bounded structural target, preserved-behavior requirement, evidence paths, and relationship to the paused WU.\n613: - ACR-179 routing: run the refactor-ticket through the ACR-179 refactoring route, using `workflows/refactoring.md` and `agents/refactoring-orchestrator.md`.\n614: - resume: resume the paused WU only after the refactor-ticket is closed, superseded, or explicitly dispositioned by the active manager flavor with recorded evidence.\n615: - touched-file/component gate failures: this pause path does not alter touched-file/component ownership; current-WU `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` code-quality findings still revise, remediate, or decompose under the existing LOW-only rules.\n616: \n617: ## Decision Recording\n618: \n619: <!-- INTENTIONAL: \"deferred\" here is a decision-recording state category, not an unwired workflow stub. -->\n620: If a phase is skipped, narrowed, deferred, terminated for non-positive value, sent back to research on invalidated assumptions, or accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk that does not collapse the approved net-value case under the supported-surface termination rule:\n621: - record it in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent\n622: - record who made the decision\n623: - record why the change was accepted\n624: - record what risk is being accepted\n625: - record what evidence caused the termination or research re-entry\n626: \n627: For Phase 6 firstness, do not create a new residual class. If a named test-verification risk remains unverified, record it in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. If only historical or process-provenance evidence is unverifiable and the approved net-value case still holds, record the decision only as `accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk` in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent.\n628: \n629: Accepted halt residuals and accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries follow this same Decision Recording rule when they do not collapse the approved net-value case.\n630: \n631: Decision Recording, generic residual recording, and Phase 7 / Phase 8 residual / value-zero review language do NOT authorize residual acceptance of Phase 4 code-quality, Phase 6 prototype-risk, or per-component code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` verdicts. Those gate verdicts revise, remediate, or decompose per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Disposition policy` and § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`; they are never recorded as residuals. Generic residual recording remains valid for non-gate residual classes only (bounded test-verification residuals in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, process-provenance uncertainty per the Phase 6 firstness paragraph above, accepted halt residuals, accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries, and Phase 8 value-zero termination evidence).\n632: \n633: Without that record, the phase was not skipped correctly.\n634: \n635: ## Orchestrator\n636: \n637: This pipeline is dispatched by the `implementation-pipeline-orchestrator` (`gpt-xhigh`) operator at `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`. The orchestrator is the only delegated actor that walks a Work Unit through every phase, dispatches each leaf operator via the `agents` CLI, runs the three required process-tree audits, and enforces the violation-escalation policy autonomously (including Tier-1 main-branch rewinds when a violation is detected). Inlining the orchestrator role into the root conversation is itself a workflow violation because it removes the orchestration decisions from `agents trace --json` and prevents process-tree-auditor from auditing the workflow as a whole.\n638: \n639: ## Adjacent References\n640: \n641: - `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n642: - `~/ai/models/roles.md`\n643: - `~/ai/workflows/agents-cli.md`\n644: - `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`\n645: - `~/ai/conventions/git.md`\n646: - `~/ai/agents/coderabbit-operator.md`\n647: - `~/ai/workflows/pr-review.md`\n648: - `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`\n649: - `~/ai/conventions/no-backwards-compatibility.md`\n650: - `~/ai/conventions/no-deferred-stubs.md`\n651: - `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md`\n652: - `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`\n\n(End of file - total 652 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- editing files is not Tier-3\n- running tests is not Tier-3\n- opening **draft** PRs is not Tier-3\n- public promotion and other outward-facing actions follow the tiered-approval workflow\n\n## Pause For Refactor\n\nThis section owns the pause state model for existing-code refactor work discovered during an implementation WU. For the strategy boundary, cite `conventions/feature-development-workflow.md` `## Refactoring out of scope`; do not restate that rule elsewhere in this workflow.\n\n- trigger: an auditor, reviewer, or implementation step identifies existing-code structural refactor work that is outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for the current WU, and material to continuing safely.\n- pause evidence: record the current ticket id, branch, head SHA, worktree path, dirty status, failing or residual report paths, and the reason the refactor is outside touched ownership or requires decomposition.\n- ticket/backend action: use the selected ticket backend to comment the pause evidence on the current ticket and apply the available pause/blocking status or label; if no status exists, use a clear blocking comment plus link relation.\n- refactor-ticket filing: file a linked refactor-ticket with the bounded structural target, preserved-behavior requirement, evidence paths, and relationship to the paused WU.\n- ACR-179 routing: run the refactor-ticket through the ACR-179 refactoring route, using `workflows/refactoring.md` and `agents/refactoring-orchestrator.md`.\n- resume: resume the paused WU only after the refactor-ticket is closed, superseded, or explicitly dispositioned by the active manager flavor with recorded evidence.\n- touched-file/component gate failures: this pause path does not alter touched-file/component ownership; current-WU `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` code-quality findings still revise, remediate, or decompose under the existing LOW-only rules.\n\n## Decision Recording\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"deferred\" here is a decision-recording state category, not an unwired workflow stub. -->","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","text":"- editing files is not Tier-3\n- running tests is not Tier-3\n- opening **draft** PRs is not Tier-3\n- public promotion and other outward-facing actions follow the tiered-approval workflow\n\n## Pause For Refactor\n\nThis section owns the pause state model for existing-code refactor work discovered during an implementation WU. For the strategy boundary, cite `conventions/feature-development-workflow.md` `## Refactoring out of scope`; do not restate that rule elsewhere in this workflow.\n\n- trigger: an auditor, reviewer, or implementation step identifies existing-code structural refactor work that is outside touched-file/component ownership, or inside touched ownership but too large for the current WU, and material to continuing safely.\n- pause evidence: record the current ticket id, branch, head SHA, worktree path, dirty status, failing or residual report paths, and the reason the refactor is outside touched ownership or requires decomposition.\n- ticket/backend action: use the selected ticket backend to comment the pause evidence on the current ticket and apply the available pause/blocking status or label; if no status exists, use a clear blocking comment plus link relation.\n- refactor-ticket filing: file a linked refactor-ticket with the bounded structural target, preserved-behavior requirement, evidence paths, and relationship to the paused WU.\n- ACR-179 routing: run the refactor-ticket through the ACR-179 refactoring route, using `workflows/refactoring.md` and `agents/refactoring-orchestrator.md`.\n- resume: resume the paused WU only after the refactor-ticket is closed, superseded, or explicitly dispositioned by the active manager flavor with recorded evidence.\n- touched-file/component gate failures: this pause path does not alter touched-file/component ownership; current-WU `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` code-quality findings still revise, remediate, or decompose under the existing LOW-only rules.\n\n## Decision Recording\n\n<!-- INTENTIONAL: \"deferred\" here is a decision-recording state category, not an unwired workflow stub. -->\nIf a phase is skipped, narrowed, deferred, terminated for non-positive value, sent back to research on invalidated assumptions, or accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk that does not collapse the approved net-value case under the supported-surface termination rule:\n- record it in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent\n- record who made the decision\n- record why the change was accepted\n- record what risk is being accepted\n- record what evidence caused the termination or research re-entry\n\nFor Phase 6 firstness, do not create a new residual class. If a named test-verification risk remains unverified, record it in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`. If only historical or process-provenance evidence is unverifiable and the approved net-value case still holds, record the decision only as `accepted with a named unverifiable residual risk` in `${worktree_path}/DECISIONS.md` or an explicitly supplied project equivalent.\n\nAccepted halt residuals and accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries follow this same Decision Recording rule when they do not collapse the approved net-value case.\n\nDecision Recording, generic residual recording, and Phase 7 / Phase 8 residual / value-zero review language do NOT authorize residual acceptance of Phase 4 code-quality, Phase 6 prototype-risk, or per-component code-quality `MEDIUM` or `HIGH` verdicts. Those gate verdicts revise, remediate, or decompose per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Disposition policy` and § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`; they are never recorded as residuals. Generic residual recording remains valid for non-gate residual classes only (bounded test-verification residuals in `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, process-provenance uncertainty per the Phase 6 firstness paragraph above, accepted halt residuals, accepted `auditor_verdict_conflict_residual` entries, and Phase 8 value-zero termination evidence).\n\nWithout that record, the phase was not skipped correctly.\n\n## Orchestrator\n\nThis pipeline is dispatched by the `implementation-pipeline-orchestrator` (`gpt-xhigh`) operator at `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`. The orchestrator is the only delegated actor that walks a Work Unit through every phase, dispatches each leaf operator via the `agents` CLI, runs the three required process-tree audits, and enforces the violation-escalation policy autonomously (including Tier-1 main-branch rewinds when a violation is detected). Inlining the orchestrator role into the root conversation is itself a workflow violation because it removes the orchestration decisions from `agents trace --json` and prevents process-tree-auditor from auditing the workflow as a whole.\n\n## Adjacent References\n\n- `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`\n- `~/ai/models/roles.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/agents-cli.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/worktree-isolation.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/git.md`\n- `~/ai/agents/coderabbit-operator.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/pr-review.md`\n- `~/ai/workflows/tiered-approval.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/no-backwards-compatibility.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/no-deferred-stubs.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/gate-ownership.md`\n- `~/ai/conventions/audit-history.md`","lineStart":600,"lineEnd":652,"totalLines":652,"truncated":false}},"title":"../../../../ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md","time":{"start":1780750720870,"end":1780750720902}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0ccf1fa93a57f80f016a241980ca6881948ea60a84c4f7dd1f"}},"id":"prt_e9d039f43001ZHfeJ5X4czM9yJ","sessionID":"ses_162fcfe43ffedLCZB78Jqbs18W","messageID":"msg_e9d037e7c0010oAvoLG710iM9M"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1780750720927,"sessionID":"ses_162fcfe43ffedLCZB78Jqbs18W","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_SnJzKaHdDcC6tyGRL84kwBgs","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"Phase 6|per-component|code-quality|contract_path|Component declared roles","path":"/home/nes/ai/workflows","include":"implementation-pipeline.md"},"output":"Found 40 matches\n/home/nes/ai/workflows/implementation-pipeline.md:\n  Line 15:     - \"keeps tests and product code separated between Phase 6b and Phase 6c invocations\"\n\n  Line 34: This workflow sequences the implementation pipeline (orchestration), validates phase/gate/audit conditions (validator), and parses ticket/frontmatter/fenced-YAML and required-token shapes throughout Phase 0, Phase 2.5, Phase 3, Phase 4, Phase 6, and Phase 8 (parser). The R6 declared-roles expansion adds `parser` per the cohesion-auditor R5 evidence at lines 68, 320, 322, and 334.\n\n  Line 70: - keeps tests and product code separated between Phase 6b and Phase 6c invocations\n\n  Line 133: - `Phase 2.5 -> Phase 3 -> Phase 4 -> Phase 5 -> Phase 6 -> Phase 7 -> Phase 8 -> Phase 9 -> Phase 10`\n\n  Line 134: - Recursion edge: completed parent `Phase 6` component work may open a child recursion level that enters `level-open` and reuses the same Phase 6 contract/test/code path; the parent reaches `child-levels-open` only after candidate children have been considered for framing.\n\n  Line 142: `normal` is the default when `pipeline_entry_mode` is absent or explicitly `normal`. The orchestrator runs the existing Phase 0 ticket/worktree/bootstrap route and then continues through Phase 2.5, Phase 3, Phase 4, Phase 5, Phase 6, Phase 7, Phase 8, Phase 9, and Final/closure as before. It does not dispatch `~/ai/workflows/audit.md`, consume an audit bundle, import review findings, or switch modes based on stray fields.\n\n  Line 188: - Tests authored in **Phase 6b** and product code authored in **Phase 6c** **are** committed in the working repository and therefore land in the worktree. The test/code boundary is the planning-vs-product boundary.\n\n  Line 191: Recursive Phase 6 artifact references are scoped by the single key `level_id`. Any recursive Phase 6 record, output-index row, emitted test group identifier, residual reference, overlay reference, swap reference, halt reference, or child-framing reference that could collide across parent and child levels must carry `level_id`; when represented as a string artifact identifier, use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>`. This scopes references only; sibling record types named here reserve the shared scoping key without defining their schemas, lifecycle, scheduling, or halt/swap criteria, and sibling record schemas that already carry `level_id` compose by using the same scope key.\n\n  Line 192: `layer_level_id`, when used by recursive Phase 6 records, is the current layer's composed identity over `level_id`, not an independent scope key or axis; string artifact identifiers still use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>`.\n\n  Line 294: - Rule: keep the map bounded to touched-file/component ownership plus adjacent supported paths. Do not expand into a whole-system inventory, but do include pre-existing code-quality debt inside files/components the WU plans to touch so Phase 2.5 can estimate, own, or decompose it.\n\n  Line 364: - Artifacts represented by the gate-set: `planning/risk/NN-audit.md`, `planning/risk/NN-scope.md`, `planning/risk/NN-shortcut.md`, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, `planning/risk/NN-proof-risk.md`, Phase 4 code-quality outputs, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-4-join-manifest.json`, expected-process evidence, process-tree audit #1, and optional bootstrap-exception ratification.\n\n  Line 370: - `code-quality`: Phase 4 pipeline-callable code-quality over current WU evidence remains part of the Phase 4 gate-set. Only `LOW` passes unless a valid bootstrap-exception row ratifies a non-LOW aggregate under `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Bootstrap exception`.\n\n  Line 376: - Rule: preserve ACR-280 strategy selection. Supported-surface non-LOW records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_INWU` or `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SUPPORTED_SURFACE_FOLLOWUP` when those classifications apply; scope decomposition records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_SCOPE_DECOMPOSED`; code-quality non-LOW that is not ratified by bootstrap exception records `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_INWU`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_HELPER_EXTRACTION`, `STRATEGY_PHASE4_CODE_QUALITY_MOVE_AND_IMPORT`, in-place file-decomposition, or follow-up-ticket decomposition according to the current finding shape.\n\n  Line 401: - Gate: model. The orchestrator advances autonomously to Phase 6 once the hookpoints artifact is non-empty, contains the four required sections (reuse points / extension points / conflicting systems / deletion candidates), and does not invalidate the approved problem map or assumption register.\n\n  Line 403: ## Phase 6 - Implementation (required; test/code separation)\n\n  Line 413: - Rule: Phase 6 firstness evidence is the Phase 6 process-tree review plus companion artifacts. The companion artifacts are the expected-process manifest, Step 6b prompt/log, Step 6c prompt/log, Step 6b output index, Step 6b output paths, and evidence that Step 6c consumed those outputs. For ACR-247 side-channel runs, that consumption evidence is the sibling `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md` projection workflow output carried in the expected-process manifest; field detail belongs to that workflow and `process-tree-auditor`. A commit marker is optional supporting evidence only. A PR-diff test file is not required firstness evidence.\n\n  Line 415: - Rule: Phase 6 consumes the approved `planning/research/NN-problem-map.md`, approved `proposals/NN-*.md` including its test-intent track and assumption register, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md`, and `planning/research/NN-hookpoints.md`.\n\n  Line 416: - Rule: Coupling/cohesion role split. `coupling-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against integrations between components at the current recursion layer, using component pairs and their current-layer integration evidence. `cohesion-auditor.md` evidence is assessed against individual components at the current recursion layer, using per-component organization evidence to decide whether the component should remain as-is, be broken out through the existing recursive child-entry path, or contribute evidence to the current-level component-set decision. The auditors produce evidence; Phase 6 owns the component-set decision.\n\n  Line 417: - Rule: RCA-track. The red signals produced or accepted in Phase 0 are inputs to the Phase 3 test-intent track and to Phase 6b. Step 6b carries them forward (existing tests satisfy firstness when the Step 6b output index maps them to the named risk and source). Step 6c is not complete until each pre-fix red signal from Phase 0 runs green against post-fix HEAD.\n\n  Line 421: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse integration tests. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same level-scoped integration test semantics repeat for that child level's own `level_id`; integration-test evidence is not a child-entry condition or gate, and `ChildLevelFramingRecord` remains the child-entry condition.\n\n  Line 422: - Rule: Recursive Phase 6 reuse. Once a child recursion level enters `level-open`, the same Step 6a contract, Step 6b test-first, Step 6c implementation, procedural-test, swap, and audit semantics apply at that child's `level_id` where those semantics exist, with continuous-overlay review evidence carried through `audit_overlay_refs` for the audited framing record.\n\n  Line 464: - Output-index fields: approved proposal path, contract path, approved `problem map` path, `planning/risk/NN-supported-surface.md` path, hookpoint research path, Step 6b prompt path, Step 6b log path, `level_id` when recursive Phase 6 work emits level-scoped artifacts, each test-intent item, named risk, selected level, proposal or assumption-register source, emitted test file path, eval-spec path, and test/eval/test-group identifier, residual entry path when the item maps to `planning/risk/NN-test-residuals.md`, documented non-applicability reason when no test or eval spec is emitted, declared fixture source or fixture application point, procedural obligation, Step 6c evidence that discovered the obligation, emitted procedural test file path and test or test-group identifier when a procedural test was authored, or procedural residual entry path when no procedural test is emitted; residual class when no procedural test is emitted. The path `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md` remains the Step 6b output index path; recursive rows and artifact identifiers carry `level_id` and use `<level_id>:<local_artifact_id>` when a string identifier is needed.\n\n  Line 469: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype risk review is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` after Step 6c produces a passing level prototype and level behavior tests pass. The required row preserves `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-risk.md`, verdict vocabulary `LOW`, `MEDIUM`, and `HIGH`, and the existing allow/block semantics before downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord`, or Phase 7 readiness consumes the prototype. This row remains distinct from `~/ai/workflows/build-prototype.md` exploratory prototype `dossier/risk-profile.md` lifecycle.\n\n  Line 470: - Rule: Phase 6 prototype-risk verdict semantics. Only `LOW` advances Phase 6 prototype risk to downstream derivation, `PrototypeSwapRecord` production or consumption, or Phase 7 advance. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` model verdicts block advance, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n\n  Line 471: - Rule: Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review consumes the Step 6a contract, Step 6b tests or structural-verification eval specs, and `${scratch_dir}/phase6/step6b-output-index.md`; checks them against the approved proposal test-intent track before Step 6c; and writes `${planning_dir}/alignment/${wu_lower}-tests-contracts.md` with verdict vocabulary `ALIGNED`, `MISALIGNED`, and `NEEDS_REVISION`. The workflow doc is the source of truth for this model-gate review's inputs, artifact path, and verdict vocabulary; the dispatch, artifact check, verdict check, refusal behavior, audit-history append, and allow-advance condition live in the Phase 6 test-contracts alignment review sub-step in `agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md`.\n\n  Line 487: - Rule: Step 6c consumption evidence for ACR-247 side-channel runs is the manifest-declared side-file projected before dispatch by `~/ai/workflows/step6c-consumption-side-file.md`. The Step 6c prompt and log remain companion topology evidence, but model-authored `consumed:` rows, read-confirmation rows, or equivalent attestations are not load-bearing firstness evidence. Missing, malformed, contradicted, stale, unmapped, late, or wrong-scope side-file evidence does not satisfy Phase 6 firstness evidence.\n\n  Line 489: - Rule: per-component code-quality auditor fanout is represented inside `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)` for each individual component after that component's Step 6c implementation and scoped tests pass, using `component_slug` to preserve `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/dispatch-manifest.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/cohesion-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/function-classification-auditor.md`, `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/reports/push-pull-auditor.md`, and `${planning_dir}/code-quality/${wu_lower}-${component_slug}/aggregate-code-quality.md`. The gate-set dispatches or verifies the whole touched component rows before that component closes into the aggregate diff consumed by downstream review. This per-component fanout does NOT replace Phase 8 PR-review aggregate gates.\n\n  Line 490: - Rule: Phase 6 per-component code-quality auditors run outside the worktree. The gate-set computes or consumes `code_quality_dispatch_dir` as the nearest existing common ancestor of absolute `worktree_path` and `planning_dir`, then dispatches each code-quality child with `agents -m <model> -p ${code_quality_dispatch_dir} -f <prompt>`. Each child prompt must pass absolute `worktree_path`, `contract_path=${planning_dir}/contracts/${wu_lower}-${slug}.md`, and `proposal_path=${planning_dir}/proposals/${wu_lower}-${wu_id}.md`. Every selected child auditor must read the Step 6a contract before scoring declared roles, adapter declarations, intrinsic-surface declarations, proof, or validation context; missing or unreadable contract evidence is `BLOCKED`, not generic scoring fallback.\n\n  Line 491: - Rule: per-component code-quality verdict semantics. The per-component code-quality auditor fanout is pipeline-callable and blocking. Only `LOW` per-component aggregate verdict allows that component to close into the aggregate diff consumed by Phase 7 CodeRabbit. `MEDIUM` and `HIGH` per-component verdicts block component closure, trigger remediation/revise from current evidence, and are never accepted as a residual, a `NEEDS_INPUT` user-disposition choice, or a stable allow-advance state. After two consecutive non-converging remediation rounds, the WU decomposes autonomously per `~/ai/conventions/code-quality.md` § `Oscillation signals WU-too-large`: decompose is not `NEEDS_INPUT`-mediated, the orchestrator records the oscillation evidence in `${planning_dir}/audit-history.md`, files the smaller sub-tickets through the project's ticket operator, and returns `DECOMPOSED` to the root caller instead of attempting a third remediation pass.\n\n  Line 493: - Rule: Post-prototype internal contract derivation is precisely one layer deep at the current recursion level. The derivation output may name only the immediate internal components needed to satisfy what the passing prototype found at that level; it must not include nested sub-components, grandchild components, or any multi-layer component hierarchy in the same derivation pass. Deeper decomposition happens only through recursive child entry and recursive Phase 6 reuse after an accepted immediate component opens a child level with its own `level_id`.\n\n  Line 503: - Rule: `CouplingDecision` records component-set evolution from coupling evidence. It is recorded adjacent to the current `LevelComponentSet` evidence, or adjacent to the candidate component inventory when no `LevelComponentSet` has been accepted yet. Required fields are `level_id`, `source_level_component_set_ref`, `candidate_component_inventory_ref` when no accepted `LevelComponentSet` exists, `coupling_audit_report_refs`, `cohesion_audit_report_refs` when the decision depends on per-component organization evidence, `verdict`, `changed_component_refs`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, `new_abstraction_component_refs`, `integration_test_refs`, `rationale_evidence_refs`, and `defer_to_prototype_risk`.\n\n  Line 505: - Rule: Evidence-bearing halt rule: a level halts only when candidate splits earn granularity under none of the `halt_basis` options: `clarify contracts`, `reduce accidental coupling`, `expose a design challenge`, `improve evidence`, `lower meaningful risk`. The `halt_basis` field records option-level evidence showing why each listed option was unsatisfied. The canonical halt record path is `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-halt-record.md`. The implementation-pipeline orchestrator checks this rule procedurally per `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § `#### Phase 6 halt-state transition gate`.\n\n  Line 509: - Rule: Process-tree review: after Step 6c completes and before Phase 7, dispatch `apply-gate-set(caller_mode=implementation-phase-6)`. The operator-owned expected-process manifest and process-tree audit #2 must prove separate Step 6b and Step 6c invocations, timing order, Step 6b output index presence, Step 6b output paths, ACR-247 side-channel consumption evidence carried by the expected-process manifest for those outputs, test-contract alignment, prototype risk review, per-component code-quality, the derivation record or no-split / rejection subsection when a derivation trigger fires, `HaltRecord` or halt/no-split evidence when halt logic fires, procedural handoff evidence for Step 6c-discovered obligations, and `CouplingDecision` evidence with rerun/current `integration_test_refs` when coupling examination changes or confirms the current component inventory. Missing, malformed, mismatched, stale, late, wrong-scope side-file evidence, or a currentness mismatch under `~/ai/conventions/apply-gate-set-currentness.md` § `Currentness key schema` and § `Row-level re-verification` is blocking. A returned `BLOCKED`, `NEEDS_INPUT`, `MEDIUM`, `HIGH`, `STALE_REFUSAL`, or process-tree blocking row prevents Phase 7; the affected subtree is rerun or repaired before downstream consumption.\n\n  Line 510: - Rule: Phase 6 -> Phase 7 `PrototypeSwapRecord` boundary: this rule sits after Step 6c's process-tree review and before Phase 7; Phase 6 still has exactly three sub-steps, Step 6a, Step 6b, and Step 6c, so this is not a Step 6d. Before Phase 7 can advance or any optional PR-mode review can consume the diff, every WU or level scope with a level prototype must produce a `PrototypeSwapRecord` at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; a WU or level scope without a level prototype must place an explicit non-applicability statement at that same path, because a missing artifact is not equivalent to non-applicability. The record must contain `component_refs`, `component_test_results`, `procedural_test_results`, `level_behavior_test_results`, `removed_or_retired_refs`, and `audit_overlay_refs`, with `level_id` and `prototype_ref` additional to the six ticket fields, plus a terminal-state field whose allowed values are `consumed`, `non-applicable`, and `superseded`. Each `audit_overlay_refs` entry must identify the artifact path and the verdict or currentness evidence proving the artifact applies to the swapped component inventory. The canonical implementation-pipeline workflow forbids the implementation-pipeline orchestrator from advancing past the Phase 7 readiness checks on a diff that has no `PrototypeSwapRecord` or explicit non-applicability statement at `${planning_dir}/risk/${wu_lower}-prototype-swap-record.md`; Phase 7 dispatch cannot advance and the optional review operator cannot consume that diff. This rule authorizes no new operators and no new workflow file, and it does not define overlay scheduling, which belongs to sibling NES-270. This prohibition is implemented by the `Pre-dispatch swap-record gate` in `~/ai/agents/implementation-pipeline-orchestrator.md` § Phase 7, which the orchestrator agent executes procedurally. Parallel prototype code cannot remain as shadow implementation unless the proposal at the level scope has named retained ...\n  Line 532: - The Phase 8 gate-set inventory is test-audit review, multi-concern PR review, justification PR review, commit hygiene, Phase 8 actual-diff code-quality, proof-risk and validation-integrity runtime-claim transport where applicable, supported-surface inventory-resolution while ACR-286 remains open, currentness rechecks against prior joins, `${planning_dir}/risk/phase-8-j